1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back. This is where a subtle, expensive class of bugs lives.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A model states a wrong answer with the same confidence as a right one,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
and if you read that confidence as correctness, the mistake flows straight into whatever you

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
build next. This lesson covers why to distrust confident output and how to verify it.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's start with three truths about model confidence, because getting these wrong is

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
the root of a lot of production incidents. First, confidence isn't correctness. A fluent,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
self-assured answer isn't evidence that it's right. The tone tells you nothing about the accuracy.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Second, wrong sounds exactly like right. A model states a false claim with the same certainty it

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
states a true one. There's no built-in hedge, no little tremor in the voice when it's mistaken.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Third, verify from outside. If correctness matters, it has to come from grounding,

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
from a citation, or from validation, never from how sure the response happens to sound.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Hold on to those three and you stop using the one signal that feels convincing but means nothing.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
So why does confidence feel so convincing when it's such a bad signal? It comes down to how

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
generation actually works. The model produces the most plausible next token, given everything

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
so far. Plausible, not verified. It's optimizing for text that reads well and fits the pattern.

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
And a confident register is just one more pattern it learned to produce. That register gets generated

17
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
the same way, whether the underlying claim is true or false. Here's the counterintuitive part.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Detail and specificity, exact numbers, named steps, precise sounding citations,

19
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
actually make a wrong answer look more credible, not less. So the more polished and specific a

20
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
response is, the more carefully you have to check it. Fluency and truth are simply not the same

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,000
thing. Let me draw the line sharply between an answer that sounds right and one that is right.

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Sounds right means fluent, detailed, self-assured. And the key insight is that this style is

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
generated the exact same way, whether the content is true or false. So on its own, it proves nothing.

24
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Is right means something different. The claim is backed by a source you can open and check,

25
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
or a value you computed, or a validation gate it passed. Correctness there is established

26
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
outside the model, not inferred from its tone. The practical move is to stop asking,

27
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
does this sound authoritative? And start asking, what backs this up that I could actually verify?

28
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,000
That single shift in question is most of the discipline. So how do you actually ground an

29
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
answer? For anything that matters, you make the model show its work against something real,

30
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
rather than taking its word. Three concrete moves. Have it cite the source or the document

31
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
it pulled a claim from, so you can go verify the claim at its origin. Prefer a computed or

32
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
looked up value over one the model recalled from memory. Because a calculation or a database read

33
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
is checkable, in a way a recollection isn't. And ask it to self-check its answer against your

34
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
criteria before it finishes, a step that reliably catches its own errors, especially on math and

35
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
code. Each of these swaps the confidence signal, which means nothing, for evidence you can actually

36
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:35,000
inspect. When the stakes are real, when output drives money, data changes, or anything hard to

37
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
undo, layer four guardrails. First, ground the claim. Require a citation or a source the model

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
can point to, so the answer rests on something you can open and check. Second, cross-check. Verify a

39
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,000
critical value a second way, recompute it, look it up, or run a second pass before you act on it.

40
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Third, validate semantics. Run a gate that judges whether the output is complete and plausible,

41
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
not merely whether it's well formed. Fourth, gate the action. Put a human or an automated check

42
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:20,000
between the confident output and any irreversible step. Ground, cross-check, validate, gate. Four layers

43
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:27,000
so no single confident mistake reaches production. Let's zero in on the failure mode that skepticism

44
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:35,000
is really built to catch, the silent one. A silent failure returns success while the content is wrong

45
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
or incomplete, and it's dangerous precisely because none of your normal safety nets see it.

46
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:47,000
No exception fires, so a try slash accept block wraps around it and catches nothing. The status

47
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,000
code comes back fine, so your error handling waves it right through. Everything at the plumbing level

48
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
looks healthy, and the bad answer proceeds. The only thing that catches this is a semantic check

49
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
aimed at the content itself, asking whether the answer is actually complete and correct.

50
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:12,000
So treat a clean status as necessary but never sufficient. A response that did an error isn't the

51
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
same as a response that's right. So if confidence can't tell you an answer is right, what can?

52
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Three verification sources, and it's worth naming them cleanly. First, grounding. You tie the answer

53
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
to real context. You handed the model, so a claim traces back to provided data instead of the model's

54
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:39,000
memory. Second, citation. You require the response to name where each fact came from, which turns an

55
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:45,000
assertion into something a reader can actually check. Third, validation. You programmatically

56
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
test the output against a schema or a business rule, which is what mechanically catches a wrong

57
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:58,000
but fluent answer that slipped past everything else. Grounding, citation, validation. Notice not

58
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
one of them asks how confident the model sounded. Everyone replaces tone with evidence you can inspect.

59
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Let's make skepticism operational, because a stance you only hold in your head does nothing. It has to

60
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:17,000
be wired into the flow. Start by deciding which outputs are actually high stakes, the ones driving

61
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:22,000
an action you can't easily undo, and demand grounding specifically there. You don't need

62
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
this ceremony on every trivial response. Then insert the validation gate before the output

63
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
drives any action, not after the damage is done. And when a check fails, route that response to a

64
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:41,000
fallback or a human, never to silent acceptance. The shape to internalize is generate, then verify,

65
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:48,000
then act. The broken shape, the one that burns people, is generate, then act on faith, trusting

66
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:54,000
the output because it sounded sure. Let's frame the big picture here. A model's confidence is

67
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:00,000
a style, not a signal. So it can't tell you an answer is right. Confidence tells you almost

68
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
nothing about accuracy in generation with LLMs. That's why correctness has to come from grounding,

69
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:12,000
citation, or validation, and why high stakes flows need a semantic gate to catch silent,

70
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:19,000
wrong but successful output. Verify before confident output ever drives a downstream action,

71
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and trust evidence over tone every time. That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

